Jane Austen opens _Pride and Prejudice_ with a sentence that sounds as
though it has already won the argument. Before a character has spoken,
the narrator announces what a wealthy unmarried man supposedly wants.
The claim belongs to the neighbourhood that receives him. The opening
chapter then follows a piece of news as it moves from that neighbourhood
into a household and acquires a particular purpose.#footnote[
  This note follows the 1894 George Allen edition, illustrated by Hugh Thomson.
]

#quote(block: true, quotes: true)[
  It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in
  possession of a good fortune must be in want of a wife.
]

The position of the phrase “universally acknowledged” matters. It gives
the sentence the form of a maxim and leaves the people doing the
acknowledging unnamed. The next paragraph supplies the missing social
setting by describing the families around a newly arrived man. Within
that setting the supposed truth becomes an expectation that those
families can act on #cite(<austen>, supplement: [chap. 1]).

= News at Netherfield

#image-left(
  image("assets/thomson-netherfield.jpg", alt: "Hugh Thomson’s drawing of a carriage arriving at Netherfield"),
