  Mrs. Bennet knows who has rented Netherfield, how he came to see it,
  and what his income is said to be. The sequence gives her account
  the authority of specific observation, even though most of what she
  says comes from Mrs. Long.

  Hugh Thomson's illustration for the 1894 edition puts the arrival
  itself on the page. Its caption takes a detail from Mrs. Bennet's
  speech and gives a reader an occasion to return to her account.
]

The scale of the conversation is deliberately small. A rental becomes a
matter of family strategy through the simple expectation that Mr. Bennet
will pay a call. The obstacle is access. A woman who knows enough about
the new neighbour to imagine a marriage must still persuade her husband
to make the introduction possible.

#pagebreak()

= An uneven conversation

Mr. Bennet answers his wife with a series of short questions. His
replies are responsive enough to keep her talking, and each makes her
spell out one more step in her reasoning. When he asks about Bingley's
“design in settling here,” he briefly entertains the possibility that
Bingley has a purpose of his own. The joke depends on treating Mrs.
Bennet's inference as though it were a proposition he could test.

Chapter two gives the conversation a second life. Mr. Bennet has already
made the visit he repeatedly declined to promise. When he reveals it, his
family learns that the exchange in the first chapter concealed a decision
he had already made #cite(<austen>, supplement: [chap. 2]). The opening
maxim and the domestic dialogue both reward attention to who knows what,
and to the moment at which they choose to tell.

== What the reader knows

The narrator lets the reader in on the visit before the family learns
about it. That slight lead changes the tone of the conversation. When
Elizabeth says they will meet Bingley at the assemblies, her suggestion
is reasonable given what she knows. The reader already knows that Mr.
Bennet has taken a step that makes an introduction easier. For a few
lines the suspense depends on when he will disclose a fact the narrative
has already given away.

The scene also changes how the opening maxim reads. In the first
chapter, a man appears to be available for other people to interpret.
In the second, a woman imagines an introduction while her husband
manages the information on which that introduction depends. Austen
locates the force of a familiar social rule in small decisions about
visiting, listening, and telling.

